EXT. MANGROVE SWAMP - HIDDEN OXBOW LAGOON - DAWN

Mist on black water. Tangled roots like the knuckles of drowned giants. A kingfisher stitches blue over the surface.

A dugout canoe drifts to the bank. DESTA ALEMAYEHU-VOSS (40s, cracked sunglasses, rope-burned palms, a satchel of dried fish) steps ashore. Behind her, curled in the bow, KIPP LOUGHRAN (teen, thin, a bandaged ankle) hugs his knees.

DESTA
Camp's through here.

KIPP
You said that yesterday.

DESTA
Yesterday I was lying.

She hauls the canoe up the bank. Kipp limps after, peering around.

An ironwood tree. A hammock. A fire pit with warm ash. A stack of dry kindling. A rusted pot, upturned, drying in the sun.

KIPP
Somebody lives here.

DESTA
Somebody did.

KIPP
Who?

She sets down the satchel. Takes out the fish and starts to lay them on a flat stone.

DESTA
Let's eat.

Kipp stays by the tree. He runs a finger along the bark. Fresh cuts. A tally: dozens of notches, in columns of five.

KIPP
These are new.

DESTA
(not looking up)
Don't touch things.

KIPP
How long since the last notch?

DESTA
Eat.

He turns. Looks at her. She keeps her eyes on the fish. The kingfisher flies off.

KIPP
You've been here before.

DESTA
Once.

KIPP
When?

She lifts her head. Her eyes go past him to the upturned pot, where a small boot, child-sized and rotted, sticks out from under the rim.

DESTA (CONT'D)
(very softly)
Kipp. Don't turn around.

He turns around. He sees the boot. He sees the tally. He counts the columns, lips moving, and stops at the last notch, which is shallow and fresh.

KIPP
There's one for me.

Desta says nothing. The mist closes over the water.
